# NFC NCI configuration.
#
# The ST HAL registers and drives the chip without this, so it is easy to miss --
# but the FRAMEWORK side was reading the wrong vendor's config file. With
# persist.nfc_cfg.config_file_name unset, nfc_config.cc falls back to
# libnfc-nci.conf, and the copy we ship at /vendor/etc/libnfc-nci.conf is the NXP
# one (it has NXP* keys and no ST keys), while the correct
# /vendor/etc/libnfc-nci-st.conf ships and goes unused. Symptom would be NFC that
# looks alive but gets RF/polling, eSE and UICC routing wrong.
#
# Stock sets this from system_ext/etc/init/init.st.nfc.rc, which we do not ship.
# It has to live in system.prop rather than vendor.prop: persist.nfc_cfg. is typed
# nfc_prop (plat_property_contexts:89) and vendor_init may not write it, whereas
# properties from the system build.prop are set by init itself.
#
# Deliberately NOT setting persist.nfc_vendor_extn.lib_file_name, which stock also
# sets here: it points at libnfc_vendor_extn_st.so, which this build does not ship.
persist.nfc_cfg.config_file_name=libnfc-nci-st.conf
ro.hw.nfc.vendor=st

# TELEPHONY: the two properties stock sets that this build did not.
#
# The radio now powers on (see the RFS work in 24dbc90) and the SIM reads end to
# end -- subId=1 at slot 0, iccId, imsi=230022701351273, displayName=O2.CZ,
# mcc=230 mnc=02 -- but it does not REGISTER: no signal, level=0, rsrp unset,
# carrierName="No service".
#
# Both are read by the framework, not by vendor, which is why they belong here
# rather than in vendor.prop. Values taken verbatim from stock's build.prop, not
# derived:
#
#   ro.telephony.default_network 26,26   THIS IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS. One entry
#                     per SIM slot. 26 is NETWORK_MODE_NR_LTE_GSM_WCDMA
#                     (RILConstants.java:232 -- NOT ..._CDMA_EVDO_..., which is 27),
#                     and it matches stock's own comment: "8+8 mode device on DSDS".
#                     Unset, RILConstants.java:255-258 falls back to
#                     NETWORK_MODE_WCDMA_PREF = 0 -- no LTE and no NR at all.
#
#   ril.subscription.types       NV,RUIM  Set for stock parity only. NOTHING READS
#                     IT: a whole-tree grep and a grep over every file in the stock
#                     dump find it in build.prop and nowhere else -- no binary, no
#                     qcril library. It is Android-4-era CDMA/RUIM cargo. Do not
#                     credit it if registration starts working. (Stock is
#                     self-inconsistent: product/etc/build.prop says RUIM,
#                     system/build.prop says NV,RUIM; system wins by load order.)
#
# NOT SET: ro.telephony.esim_slot_id, which stock DOES set to 1 at
# product/etc/build.prop:132. Left out because nothing in AOSP or LineageOS reads
# that name -- it is a Motorola-framework property, and we do not ship that
# framework. Revisit if eSIM is ever wanted.
#
# CAVEAT ON TESTING: SubscriptionManagerService uses PREFERRED_NETWORK_MODE to seed
# a subscription's allowed network types AT INSERT. subId=1 is already provisioned
# on this device, so the new value may not reach the existing row without
# re-provisioning the subscription. Do NOT read "still no signal" as "the property
# did nothing" -- check the subscription row, or wipe and re-insert it.
#
# UNVERIFIED: whether these are sufficient for registration. They are the cheapest
# remaining difference from stock on this path; if the radio still finds nothing,
# the next leads are the ~11 persist.vendor.radio.* stock sets that we do not
# (apm_sim_not_pwdn, sib16_support, custom_ecc, rat_on=combine, ...), and the 27
# of 72 missing /vendor/etc/qcril_database/upgrade/*.sql (vendor.radio.db_state=3).
ril.subscription.types=NV,RUIM
ro.telephony.default_network=26,26
